# Task 1 staged implementation report

Status: implemented in separate proposed copies; ready for independent review. Not applied. No implementation commit exists. Task 2/3 and remote calls remain unperformed and unapproved where applicable.

## Package

`originals/` contains exact source preimage bytes; `proposed/` contains the integrated proposed source using repository-relative paths. `source-preimages.json` and `proposed-hashes.json` enumerate all seven SHA256 identities. `implementation.patch` uses canonical repository-relative a/b headers. `verification-output.txt` contains exact static results; `verification-command.json` records the Ruff argument array. `live-preimage-verification.json` records all seven live sources still matching their original snapshots at packaging. No other source was changed. These receipts do not replace the fresh preimage check required before application.

## Integrated behavior

- contracts.py and manifest.py independently accept provider-backed development only with disabled development semantics and zero retries/repair. Optional USD semantics and all existing capability checks are unchanged. No apply/qualification/replay permission was added.
- development_preparation.py splits common development scope from controller-only exchange. A distinct ProviderDevelopmentAdapterConfigV5 binds the host identities, gateway/ledger/audit identities, fixed OPENROUTER_API_KEY handle, and policy_only export. The old controller config definition/serialized shape is unchanged. Config loading selects its exact type from the authenticated manifest, not file shape or a caller mode flag. The shared development factory accepts both exact types and validates manifest/root/mode and concrete graph identity.
- cli.py factors the existing paid construction and exact graph verification for reuse. Production wrappers retain production scope and full export. Development injects that same ledger-backed invoker into the existing shared runtime with policy_only export. No transport, candidate engine or accounting subsystem is duplicated.
- operations.py derives paid identities whenever a provider is present, selects the appropriate config, and requires response_directory only for controller mode (rejects it for paid mode). Per-round config derivation carries the correct path/credential handle fields. The started/no-journal exemption remains exclusively provider-free controller development; paid uncertainty cannot silently restart. Existing reservation reconciliation, owner hashes, original deadlines and budget stopping are reused unchanged.
- runtime.py admits controller terminal provenance only for provider-free development; paid development requires LedgerRoleTerminalAuthorityV5. Existing pending controller gate already requires provider=None and remains unchanged. Existing paid reconciliation remains unchanged.
- summary.py and development history use original saved launch/config authority, then the existing local run verifier selects controller package authentication or paid ledger authentication based on manifest.provider. The controller-only helper now explicitly rejects paid authority. Resource history already reconstructs config.export_mode, so paid development preserves policy-only source authentication without additional changes.

## Exact preparation interfaces after later reviewed application

The existing `import-development --input-file ABSOLUTE_JSON [--validate-only]` input may include `provider` or omit it/use null for controller mode. A non-null provider uses the existing canonical ProviderCapabilitiesV5 JSON shape, with all dataclass keys present: model, maximum_role_calls, maximum_total_tokens, maximum_output_tokens_per_role, maximum_usd, automatic_retries, schema_repair_calls, price_upper_bound, input_token_overhead_upper_bound. Decimal fields use canonical serialized decimal strings; maximum_usd/price_upper_bound may be null for audited-cost-only mode. Nonzero retries/repair reject before first import write. No model, token value, price or user budget is chosen by this patch. Existing panel/data/source/caps inputs and real parent authentication remain required. Validation/preparation results disclose the provider capabilities.

`prepare-development` uses the existing real path arguments. Controller mode still requires --response-directory; provider mode omits it and rejects a supplied directory. It creates a distinct config with a fixed credential HANDLE, never the value. `run-campaign` and `resume-campaign` dispatch by authenticated scope and provider/config authority using the existing execution loop. `verify-run` and `summarize` use the development config loader and shared history verifier. Standalone production run/resume remain production-only. No mixed staged/live CLI execution was attempted.

## Assumption/consumer audit

Resolved both independent provider prohibitions (contracts and manifest builder), development common/controller helper scope, config/factory type and path selection, production-only paid composition reuse, development terminal selection, and the paid/no-journal recovery exemption. Retained controller_roles.py provider-free guard, fixture authority checks, provider-free pending behavior, evaluator/container-protocol provisional data/panel restrictions, disabled semantics, production standalone restrictions, and optional monetary policy. Inspected production_provider.py ledger construction/reservation/accounting and provider.py role types/serialization: their paid machinery is already scope-neutral and needs no direct consumer edit. Existing `_verify_local_run` provider-present branch already authenticates ledger receipts; resource history already uses export_mode. No direct consumers outside the planned seven require edits from this source assessment.

## Verification and limitations

Scoped Ruff passed on seven proposed paths. Every proposed file compiled in memory without imports. Added patch lines passed whitespace review; all source snapshots and live preimage matches passed. No tests were read/created/modified/run; no synthetic probes, application imports/CLI, credentials, provider/network/Docker calls, staging in Git, commits, hooks, or campaign/artifact writes occurred. This staging package is source evidence only, not proof of runtime behavior.

Independent review is still required. The image closure changes (at least contracts/runtime) require rebuilt/sealed exact evaluator authority and fresh authentic baseline reports before a new campaign. Actual call-free preparation and later explicitly approved provider execution remain controller-owned after live campaign cleanup and application. The sole remaining operational choices are the model/destination, finite token/call/time caps, optional USD policy/prices, source/evidence disclosure and explicit external authorization. No speculative accounting or policy behavior changes are included.

